---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildFAQSchema } from "../../lib/seo";

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    question: "What is the key to a strong IELTS opinion essay?",
    answer:
      "The key is to make your opinion clear early, keep it consistent, and support it with well-developed reasons instead of short, general points.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I discuss both sides in an opinion essay?",
    answer:
      "Only if the question asks you to. In a standard opinion essay, your main job is to defend your own position clearly and directly.",
  },
  {
    question: "How many reasons should I give in an opinion essay?",
    answer:
      "Two strong reasons are usually enough, it is better to develop two ideas well than to mention four ideas briefly.",
  },
];

const pageStructuredData = [
  buildBreadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", path: "/blog/" },
    { name: "IELTS Writing Task 2 Opinion Essay", path: "/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-opinion-essay/" },
  ]),
  buildFAQSchema(pageFaqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="IELTS Writing Task 2 Opinion Essay | Clear Position and Strong Support"
  description="IELTS opinion essay guidance with a clear structure, stronger support, and practical advice for writing a direct Band 7 style response."
  type="article"
  keywords="IELTS opinion essay, IELTS Task 2 opinion essay, IELTS writing opinion structure"
  structuredData={pageStructuredData}
>
  <article class="section guide-article">
    <a class="guide-back-link" href="/blog">Back to blog</a>

    <header class="guide-header">
      <span class="guide-tag ic-eyebrow">IELTS writing guide</span>
      <h1 class="ic-display" style="margin: 0;">IELTS Writing Task 2 opinion essay</h1>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span>March 17, 2026</span>
        <span>7 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-lead">
      <p>
        An opinion essay looks simple, but many students lose marks because the opinion is
        not strong enough. The introduction sounds neutral, the body paragraphs drift into
        different directions, or the essay repeats the same point with different words.
      </p>
      <p>
        A strong opinion essay is direct. The reader should know what you believe within
        the first few lines.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-grid guide-grid-2">
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2 class="ic-display">What this question is really testing</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>Can you take a clear position?</li>
          <li>Can you support that position with useful reasons?</li>
          <li>Can you keep the whole essay moving in one direction?</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2 class="ic-display">What usually goes wrong</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>The writer sounds unsure in the introduction.</li>
          <li>The two body paragraphs repeat the same idea.</li>
          <li>The examples are too general to be convincing.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">A simple opinion essay plan</h2>
      <div class="guide-steps">
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Introduction</h3>
            <p>Paraphrase the topic and state your opinion clearly. Do not wait until the conclusion.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Body paragraph 1</h3>
            <p>Give your strongest reason first. Explain it and support it with one relevant example.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Body paragraph 2</h3>
            <p>Give a different reason. Avoid repeating the same idea with slightly different words.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Conclusion</h3>
            <p>Restate your opinion briefly. Keep it short and confident.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">Example of a clear position</h2>
      <div class="guide-example">
        <p><strong>Question:</strong> Do the advantages of remote work outweigh the disadvantages?</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Clear opinion:</strong> In my view, the advantages of remote work do
          outweigh the disadvantages because it gives workers more flexibility and reduces
          wasted commuting time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="guide-note" style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">
        <p>
          This works because the writer chooses a side and previews the two body ideas at
          the same time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">How to choose better reasons</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>Choose reasons that are easy to explain, not reasons that only sound impressive.</li>
        <li>Make sure the two body ideas are different from each other.</li>
        <li>Use examples that show a real effect, not just a vague opinion.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">Common mistakes</h2>
      <table class="guide-mistake-table">
        <tr>
          <th>Mistake</th>
          <th>Better move</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The essay gives an opinion, then sounds neutral later.</td>
          <td>Keep the same position all the way through the essay.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Both body paragraphs support almost the same reason.</td>
          <td>Choose two clearly different arguments.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The example is too general.</td>
          <td>Show a clear real-world effect or realistic situation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The conclusion introduces a new idea.</td>
          <td>Only restate the opinion and the logic already used.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">Next step</h2>
      <div class="guide-links">
        <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-discussion-essay">
          <strong>Read the discussion essay guide</strong>
          <span>See how the structure changes when you need to show both views.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/ai-feedback">
          <strong>Try AI writing feedback</strong>
          <span>Get feedback on whether your position stays clear and well supported.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/ielts/writing">
          <strong>Go to the IELTS writing hub</strong>
          <span>Move to the next writing task or supporting lesson.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">FAQ</h2>
      <div class="guide-faq">
        {pageFaqs.map((item) => (
          <details class="card">
            <summary>{item.question}</summary>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--s-2); margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <section class="section-sm" style="max-width: 640px; margin: 0 auto; text-align: center;">
    <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid #ddd; margin-bottom: var(--s-5);" />
    <p class="muted">Want a CLB 9 writing system you can actually follow?</p>
    <a class="btn" href="/celpip/writing">Get the system</a>
  </section>
</Layout>
